\subsection{Preprocessing}
The text was cleaned by removing URLs and HTML tags and standardizing whitespace. I do not remove punctuation, special characters, or symbols, because these are important signal for distinguishing human-written from AI-generated text.

\textbf{Truncation by number of syllables.} The two encoders use different tokenizers, so the same number of tokens covers different amounts of content: with the same 256-token limit, PhoBERT processed about 40\% more content than BamiBERT, because each PhoBERT token is typically a complete compound word. To remove this source of variation, all text was truncated to the first 225 syllables before tokenization, so both encoders receive the same text. This length was chosen so that the token limits do not remove any further content: 99.58\% of the articles fit within PhoBERT's 256 tokens, and all but 2 of the 5,000 articles fit within BamiBERT's 512 tokens. Since 98.3\% of the texts have at least 225 syllables, truncation also virtually removes the length difference between human-written and AI-generated texts, preventing the model from learning length instead of writing style.

\subsection{Text Representation}
I used two pre-trained Vietnamese models as backbones: PhoBERT-base~\cite{phobert} and BamiBERT~\cite{bamibert}. Each backbone consists of a corresponding tokenizer and encoder, and the two backbones differ in their input preparation steps. PhoBERT was pre-trained on word-segmented text, so the input is word-segmented using the underthesea library~\cite{underthesea} before tokenization; the syllables of a word are joined by underscores and treated as a single unit (e.g., \emph{trí\_tuệ nhân\_tạo}). BamiBERT accepts raw text and tokenizes it directly using its BPE vocabulary. The length limit is $L = 256$ tokens for PhoBERT and $L = 512$ tokens for BamiBERT.

For a text of $L$ tokens or fewer, the encoder produces one vector per token at its final layer, each with $d = 768$ values for both encoders. The encoder is \textbf{frozen} (evaluation mode, no gradients), so these token vectors are computed once for the whole dataset and cached (fp16) for both classification models and all folds.

\subsection{Classification Models}
Both classification models (heads) take as input the token vectors produced by the frozen encoder (one 768-dimensional vector per token) and output the probability that the text is human-written or AI-generated. Because texts have different numbers of tokens, each model first condenses the sequence of token vectors into a single summary vector of fixed length, ignoring padding tokens. The two models differ only in how they do this. Both are initialized randomly and are the only components that are trained.

\textbf{CNN.} Following Kim~\cite{kim2014}, the CNN slides small filters along the text, each covering a few consecutive tokens (kernel size $k$ = 2, 3, or 4), to detect local patterns similar to n-grams. There are 128 filters for each size ($F$ = 128). For each filter, only its strongest response over the whole text is kept (max-over-time pooling), so the result tells whether the pattern appears anywhere in the text, regardless of its position. The results of all 384 filters together form the summary vector.

\textbf{BiLSTM.} The BiLSTM~\cite{lstm,bilstm} reads the token vectors twice, once from the beginning to the end and once from the end to the beginning, so that every position takes both the preceding and the following context into account. The summary vector is made of the final states of the two readings (128 values each, hidden size $h$ = 128), i.e., 256 values in total.

\textbf{Output layer and loss function.} For each head, the summary vector passes through dropout (rate 0.1), a linear layer, and a softmax that turns the result into the probability of each class. The model is trained with the cross-entropy loss.

% =====================================================================
\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\subsection{Data Split}
I used group-stratified 5-fold cross-validation (StratifiedGroupKFold). Each AI-rewritten article was in the same group as its original article (matched by URLs), so an original article and its rewritten version never appear on opposite sides, one in the training set and the other in the test set; stratifying by text type keeps the proportion of the three types consistent across folds. Each fold uses 20\% of the data as the test set; from the remaining 80\%, a portion equivalent to 10\% of the total data (also divided by group) is set aside as the validation set, so each fold has a training/validation/test ratio of approximately 70/10/20. Each sample appears exactly once in the test set, so out-of-fold predictions cover all 5,000 samples.

\subsection{Implementation Details}
Four models sharing the same training hyperparameters (Table~\ref{tab:hparams}). The models were trained for 30 epochs without early stopping; after at each epoch, the macro-F1 score on the validation set is calculated, and the weights from the epoch with the highest macro-F1 score are retained for evaluation on the test set. The experiments were run on an NVIDIA RTX 2070 GPU (8~GB) using PyTorch 2.7.1 (CUDA 11.8).

\begin{table}[t]
  \centering
  \caption{Common training hyperparameters for the four models.}
  \label{tab:hparams}
  \setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
  \begin{tabular}{ll}
    \toprule
    Hyperparameter & Value \\
    \midrule
    $S$ (number of leading syllables kept) & 225 \\
    $L$ (maximum number of tokens) & PhoBERT 256, BamiBERT 512 \\
    $d$ (encoder hidden size) & 768 \\
    Optimizer & AdamW \cite{adamw} \\
    $\eta$ (learning rate) & $10^{-3}$ \\
    $\lambda$ (weight decay) & 0.01 \\
    $B$ (batch size) & 32 \\
    $E$ (number of epochs) & 30, no early stopping \\
    $\tau$ (gradient-norm clipping threshold) & 1.0 \\
    $p$ (dropout rate) & 0.1 \\
    $k$ (CNN kernel sizes) & 2, 3, 4 \\
    $F$ (CNN filters per $k$) & 128 \\
    $h$ (BiLSTM hidden size per direction) & 128 \\
    BiLSTM layers & 1 \\
    $s$ (seed of fold $f$) & $42 + f$ \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}
The AI class is considered the positive class. I use Accuracy, Precision, Recall, and F1 (the harmonic mean of Precision and Recall) for the positive class. The metrics are calculated on the test set of each fold and reported as the mean $\pm$ standard deviation across 5 folds. I also report accuracy separately for each text type.

% =====================================================================
\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Overall Performance}
Table~\ref{tab:main} presents the results of the 5-fold cross-validation. BamiBERT + CNN achieved the highest results in Accuracy (95.42\%), Recall (93.44\%), and F1 (95.33\%). PhoBERT + CNN followed closely with an Accuracy of 94.84\% and an F1 of 94.69\%, and had the highest Precision (97.61\% vs. 97.30\%). The standard deviation of BamiBERT + CNN is also the smallest (Accuracy $\pm$0.42 compared to $\pm$0.76 for PhoBERT + CNN), indicating that the model is more stable when the training data changes.

\begin{table}[t]
  \centering
  \caption{Results of 5-fold cross-validation (\%, mean $\pm$ standard deviation). Best values in bold.}
  \label{tab:main}
  \setlength{\tabcolsep}{0.8pt}
  \begin{tabular}{lcccc}
    \toprule
    Model & Accuracy & Precision & Recall & F1 \\
    \midrule
    PhoBERT + CNN     & \pmv{94.84}{0.76} & \pmv{\textbf{97.61}}{1.70} & \pmv{91.96}{1.11} & \pmv{94.69}{0.75} \\
    PhoBERT + BiLSTM  & \pmv{90.60}{0.58} & \pmv{94.13}{1.59} & \pmv{86.64}{1.74} & \pmv{90.21}{0.64} \\
    BamiBERT + CNN    & \pmv{\textbf{95.42}}{0.42} & \pmv{97.30}{0.98} & \pmv{\textbf{93.44}}{0.48} & \pmv{\textbf{95.33}}{0.40} \\
    BamiBERT + BiLSTM & \pmv{91.30}{0.83} & \pmv{93.73}{1.44} & \pmv{88.56}{2.08} & \pmv{91.05}{0.93} \\
    \midrule
    {\scriptsize BamiBERT + CNN$\|$BiLSTM} & \pmv{95.02}{0.35} & \pmv{96.58}{1.07} & \pmv{93.36}{0.68} & \pmv{94.94}{0.33} \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

The impact of the classification model is significantly greater than that of the encoder. For both encoders, the CNN outperforms the BiLSTM by approximately 4 percentage points in accuracy (PhoBERT: 94.84\% vs. 90.60\%; BamiBERT: 95.42\% vs. 91.30\%). Conversely, when keeping the classification model constant, replacing PhoBERT with BamiBERT only increased Accuracy by 0.58 points (CNN) and 0.70 points (BiLSTM). This increase is small compared to the standard deviation across folds (0.42--0.83 points), so it is not sufficient to conclusively state that BamiBERT is better than PhoBERT; conversely, the approximately 4-point difference between CNN and BiLSTM is much larger than the standard deviation.

The last row of Table~\ref{tab:main} is an extension with a combined head, described in Section~\ref{sec:ext}.

\subsection{Extension: Combined CNN and BiLSTM Head}
\label{sec:ext}
Since the performance of BamiBERT is outperformed PhoBERT, so I want to test whether the two classification models complement each other, I added a third head to BamiBERT, consisting of a CNN branch and a BiLSTM branch---as described above---running in parallel on the same token vectors. Their two summary vectors are joined into one (640 values in total) and trained jointly using a single loss function. This head is not part of the four main combinations and is evaluated separately.

With the combined CNN$\|$BiLSTM head (last row of Table~\ref{tab:main}), evaluated on the same folds and seeds, Accuracy is 95.02\% versus 95.42\% for BamiBERT + CNN, a difference within the standard deviation across folds, while it outperforms both BiLSTM models by about 4 points. Thus combining the two heads only reaches the level of the standalone CNN.

\subsection{Performance by Text Type}
Table~\ref{tab:bytype} breaks down accuracy by three text types. The AI-Generated group has nearly reached saturation (99.36\% for both CNN models), and the Human group shows comparable accuracy between the two encoders (97.72\% versus 97.40\% for CNN; PhoBERT performs slightly better, consistent with its higher Precision in Table~\ref{tab:main}). The gap between the two encoders lies almost entirely in the AI-Rewritten group, the most challenging category: BamiBERT gains 2.96 points over PhoBERT with the CNN head (87.52\% vs. 84.56\%) and 3.92 points with the BiLSTM head (79.20\% vs. 75.28\%). This is the source of the higher Recall in BamiBERT. 
\begin{table}[t]
  \centering
  \caption{Accuracy by text type (\%, mean $\pm$ standard deviation over 5 folds).}
  \label{tab:bytype}
  \setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
  \begin{tabular}{lccc}
    \toprule
    Model & Human & AI-Gen. & AI-Rewr. \\
    \midrule
    PhoBERT + CNN     & \pmv{\textbf{97.72}}{1.69} & \pmv{\textbf{99.36}}{0.61} & \pmv{84.56}{2.72} \\
    PhoBERT + BiLSTM  & \pmv{94.56}{1.62} & \pmv{98.00}{0.80} & \pmv{75.28}{3.46} \\
    BamiBERT + CNN    & \pmv{97.40}{0.97} & \pmv{\textbf{99.36}}{0.78} & \pmv{\textbf{87.52}}{1.31} \\
    BamiBERT + BiLSTM & \pmv{94.04}{1.54} & \pmv{97.92}{1.25} & \pmv{79.20}{3.14} \\
    \midrule
    {\scriptsize BamiBERT + CNN$\|$BiLSTM} & \pmv{96.68}{1.08} & \pmv{99.28}{0.77} & \pmv{87.44}{1.28} \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Error Analysis}
Figure~\ref{fig:scores} shows the AI probability that BamiBERT + CNN assigns to each text type (out-of-fold, log scale, dashed line: threshold 0.5). Human text clusters near 0 and AI-generated text near 1, so the model makes few errors on these types (65 and 8 texts). AI-rewritten text has two peaks: 109 of its 156 errors have an AI probability below 0.1, i.e., the model is confident they are human-written. Overall, 137 of 229 errors (59.8\%) have a probability of 0.9 or higher on the wrong side, so errors are mostly confident ones rather than near-threshold cases; this fits the validation loss increasing while accuracy does not decrease (Loss Curves) and suggests that adjusting the decision threshold is unlikely to fix them.

Figure~\ref{fig:overlap} counts the texts that only one encoder classifies correctly with the CNN head. BamiBERT's advantage comes almost entirely from AI-Rewritten text (57 texts correct only for BamiBERT versus 20 only for PhoBERT); on Human text it is slightly worse (31 versus 39) and on AI-Generated text the two are indistinguishable (2 versus 2). In total, 90 texts are correct only for BamiBERT and 61 only for PhoBERT, so the encoders disagree on very few samples (151 of 5,000). Moreover, 136 of the 156 AI-rewritten texts misclassified by BamiBERT are also misclassified by PhoBERT, so most errors are shared and the limit may lie in this text type rather than in the encoder.

\begin{figure}[!t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_score_distribution.png}
  \caption{AI probability distribution of BamiBERT + CNN by text type (out-of-fold predictions; y-axis on a logarithmic scale; dashed line: threshold of 0.5). The title of each panel indicates the error rate for that text type.}
  \label{fig:scores}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[!t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_error_overlap.png}
  \caption{Number of texts where only BamiBERT is correct, only PhoBERT is correct, or both are incorrect, by text type (CNN head, out-of-fold predictions on 5,000 texts).}
  \label{fig:overlap}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure*}[!t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{fig_loss_cv.png}
  \caption{Cross-entropy loss by epoch on the training, validation, and test sets (average of 5 folds; the shaded area represents $\pm$1 standard deviation across folds).}
  \label{fig:loss}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Computational Cost}
Table~\ref{tab:time} reports the training and test time per fold on an RTX 2070. For the CNN head, training with BamiBERT takes about 2.1 times longer than with PhoBERT (201.56~s vs. 94.65~s); for the BiLSTM head it takes about 1.5 times longer (206.25~s vs. 133.53~s). Testing is fast for all four models, 0.58--1.29~s per fold. The main reason for the difference is that, with the same 225 syllables, BamiBERT's tokenizer generates longer token sequences, which increases the cost of both the encoder and the classification model. Extracting the features of the 5,000 texts, done once per encoder, also takes longer with BamiBERT (104.97~s vs. 40.95~s). In summary, compared to PhoBERT + CNN, BamiBERT + CNN gains 0.58 points of Accuracy and 1.48 points of Recall at roughly twice the computational cost.

\begin{table}[t]
  \centering
  \caption{Training and test time (seconds) per fold.}
  \label{tab:time}
  \setlength{\tabcolsep}{6pt}
  \begin{tabular}{lcc}
    \toprule
    Model & Train (s) & Test (s) \\
    \midrule
    PhoBERT + CNN     &  94.65 & 0.58 \\
    PhoBERT + BiLSTM  & 133.53 & 0.78 \\
    BamiBERT + CNN    & 201.56 & 1.16 \\
    BamiBERT + BiLSTM & 206.25 & 1.29 \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Loss Curves}
Figure~\ref{fig:loss} shows the cross-entropy loss per epoch, averaged over 5 folds. The training loss of all four models converges to near 0 after about 9--12 epochs, whereas the validation and test loss do not follow: for the CNN it is lowest around epochs 2--3 and then stabilizes near 0.2; for the BiLSTM it increases from about 0.22 to 0.45--0.55 with larger fluctuation across folds. The two encoders give nearly identical curves with the same classification model, so overfitting mainly stems from the classification model rather than the encoder. This is why I retained the weights of the epoch with the highest validation macro-F1 instead of the final epoch; the test curve is shown for observation only and is not used for model selection.



% =====================================================================
\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\textbf{BamiBERT vs. PhoBERT.} When both encoders process the same content, BamiBERT outperforms PhoBERT, but by a narrow margin: the difference is only about 0.6--0.7 points in Accuracy, which is small compared to the standard deviation across folds (0.4--0.8 points); therefore, additional data or multiple runs with different seeds are needed to confirm this result. BamiBERT's advantage is concentrated in the AI-Rewritten group, where the content remains the author's own and only the phrasing has changed. One plausible explanation is that BamiBERT represents raw text at the subword level, thus preserving details regarding word choice and punctuation that PhoBERT's tokenization step might lose; however, this hypothesis requires further validation.

\textbf{CNN vs. BiLSTM.} CNN outperforms BiLSTM with both encoders and shows fewer signs of overfitting. Since the encoder's hidden states already carry bidirectional context, extra sequential modeling by the BiLSTM brings little benefit, while its larger number of parameters makes it prone to overfitting on about 3,600 training samples per fold. A CNN with max-pooling only needs to detect a few characteristic local patterns anywhere in the text, which suits AI-generated text signals that appear in phrases and local expressions.

\textbf{Combining CNN and BiLSTM.} The combined head achieved 95.02\% accuracy, comparable to the standalone CNN (95.42\%, within the standard deviation across folds), with no improvement on AI-Rewritten text (87.44\% vs. 87.52\%). An initial 70/15/15 split suggested a small advantage over the CNN (95.04\% vs. 94.39\% on 766 samples), but it disappeared in cross-validation, showing that a single split can mislead. The BiLSTM branch added no useful information to the CNN on frozen features and increased training cost.

\textbf{Limitations.} This study has several limitations. First, the data comes from only one domain (IT news), and the AI-generated text is sourced from only two LLMs (Gemini and DeepSeek, free versions), so the ability to generalize to other domains and to other LLMs such as ChatGPT or Claude has not been evaluated. Second, the encoder was used only in its frozen form; fine-tuning the entire model might alter the rankings. Third, hyperparameters were kept uniform and thus not optimized individually for each model, and each fold ran only one seed. Finally, the model read only the first 225 syllables of each article.

% =====================================================================
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
% NOTE: in the PDF this section is titled "DISCUSSION" by mistake; the heading here stays "Conclusion".
This paper presents a controlled comparison between PhoBERT and BamiBERT as frozen feature extractors for the task of detecting AI-generated Vietnamese text, alongside two classification models: CNN and BiLSTM. On a dataset of 5,000 IT news articles, with both encoders reading the first 225 syllables of each article, BamiBERT + CNN achieved the best results (Accuracy 95.42\%, F1 95.33\%). BamiBERT outperformed PhoBERT by approximately 0.6 percentage points in accuracy when using a CNN, with its advantage concentrated in AI-rewritten text, but at roughly double the computational cost. The choice of classification model has a greater impact on the results than the choice of encoder: CNN significantly outperforms BiLSTM with both encoders, while combining CNN and BiLSTM in parallel does not improve upon the single CNN (95.02\% vs. 95.42\%).

In the future, I plan to fine-tune the entire encoder, expand the dataset to include multiple domains and various LLMs, evaluate the model on texts longer than 225 syllables, and analyze the signals the model uses to identify AI-rewritten text.

